## Framework migration: session affinity syntax

The `metadata`, `spec`, and `port` blocks keep their existing syntax. If you
configure `session_affinity_config`, use list-of-object assignment for it and
its nested `client_ip`. This allows omitted values to remain API-computed without
changing the stored list shape.

Before:

```terraform
session_affinity_config {
  client_ip {
    timeout_seconds = 300
  }
}
```

After, inside the same `spec` block:

```terraform
session_affinity_config = [{
  client_ip = [{
    timeout_seconds = 300
  }]
}]
```

An empty `session_affinity_config {}` becomes `session_affinity_config = [{}]`.
If the configuration omitted session affinity configuration entirely, keep it
omitted. The syntax change must not alter the Service's affinity settings.

With `session_affinity = "ClientIP"`, do not use `[]` for either
`session_affinity_config` or `client_ip`: Kubernetes requires a populated
configuration. Use omission, `null`, or `[{}]` for API-computed values.

### Empty collections and automatic ports

Some SDK states store `null` for explicitly empty `metadata.annotations`,
`metadata.labels`, `spec.selector`, `spec.external_ips`, or
`spec.load_balancer_source_ranges`. Keeping `{}` or `[]` in configuration can
produce a state-only normalization to an empty collection. The Service is not
patched for this normalization, but expressions that distinguish `null` from
an empty collection can change. Review dependent resources as well as the Service.

For automatic node-port allocation, omit `node_port` or set it to `null`; do not
set it to `0`. The Framework resource rejects an explicit zero when the Service
would allocate a port. Released SDK versions could allocate a port while still
planning it back to zero and exposing zero in outputs. Remove the zero setting
using the previous provider version, review any output or dependent-resource
changes, and establish a clean plan before migrating.

An explicit `target_port = ""` or `target_port = "0"` must also become omission or `null`.
Kubernetes resolves these markers to a concrete target port. The Framework rejects
these markers instead of recording a value that differs from the API.
Omitting the argument retains an existing computed target port.

For Services other than `ExternalName`, replace `cluster_ips = []` and
`ip_families = []` with omission or `null`. Kubernetes populates these lists.
The SDK could produce perpetual updates or a replacement plan for these empty
markers; establish a clean SDK plan before migration. The Framework rejects
them before API writes rather than storing empty lists over existing allocations.

## Migration from `kubernetes_service`

Terraform 1.8 or later can move an existing Service from the deprecated
`kubernetes_service` resource to `kubernetes_service_v1`:

```terraform
moved {
  from = kubernetes_service.example
  to   = kubernetes_service_v1.example
}
```

Change the existing resource declaration to `kubernetes_service_v1` and update
references to its Terraform address. Do not retain both resource declarations or
import a second copy of the same Service. Keep the provider connection pointed
at the same Kubernetes cluster, and keep the name, namespace, ports, selectors
and other service settings unchanged.

A whole-resource move also covers existing `count` or `for_each` instances when
their instance keys stay unchanged. A same-type address rename can use a
`moved` block with `kubernetes_service_v1` on both sides.

An existing `kubernetes_service_v1` resource needs no `moved` block merely to
upgrade the provider. The Kubernetes Service should retain its UID, cluster IPs
and allocated ports. Review the plan before applying: an unchanged migration must
not delete or replace the Service or change its networking settings. Stop and
investigate an unexpected replacement rather than applying it.

The reverse cross-type move to `kubernetes_service` is not supported: that
deprecated destination remains on SDKv2.

## Import

Service can be imported using its namespace and name, e.g.

```
$ terraform import kubernetes_service_v1.example default/terraform-name
```
